is":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Zion::Zion::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Zion"},"text":"for there our captors requested a song; our tormentors demanded songs of joy: “Sing us a song of Zion.”","verse":3}],"id":"Zion","label":"Zion","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Zion::Zion::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Zion","term":"Zion"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Zion","sourceTitle":"Zion"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Ps.137.1-9","r":"Ps.137.1-9","rawR":"Ps.137.1-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.137.1-9","value":"Ps.137.1-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 137"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," In response to the pain of exile (",{"href":"?bref=Ps.137.1-4","r":"Ps.137.1-4","rawR":"Ps.137.1-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.137.1-4","value":"Ps.137.1-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"137:1-4"},"), the psalmist resolves to remember Jerusalem even though the memories cause him pain (",{"href":"?bref=Ps.137.4-6","r":"Ps.137.4-6","rawR":"Ps.137.4-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.137.4-6","value":"Ps.137.4-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"137:4-6"},"). He also writes an astounding imprecation, or prayer for vengeance (",{"href":"?bref=Ps.137.7-9","r":"Ps.137.7-9","rawR":"Ps.137.7-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.137.7-9","value":"Ps.137.7-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"137:7-9"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Ps.137.1-9","refs":[{"book":"PSA","endChapter":137,"endVerse":9,"startChapter":137,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Ps.137.1-9","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PSA","sourceChapterCoverage":[137],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"3d6f5a20188b7671fe719ca162a1f8e8fc4c53a37fe09ab0dc997f135ac03773","sourceItemName":"Ps.137.1-9","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7382,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"PSA","canonicalOSIS":"Ps","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Ps"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Ps.137.1-9","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Ps.137.1-9","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Ps.137.2-4","r":"Ps.137.2-4","rawR":"Ps.137.2-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.137.2-4","value":"Ps.137.2-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"137:2-4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Because the music of Jerusalem was tied to the joys of life in the Promised Land, the Exile removed any occasion for singing joyous songs. The Babylonians wanted the Judeans to sing and dance for them, but the exiles’ grief made doing so either distasteful or impossible. The songs celebrating the Lord speak of his power and goodness, but his people were filled with doubts and questions. They hung their harps in trees, perhaps signifying the death of their joy under God’s curse (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.23","r":"Deut.21.23","rawR":"Deut.21.23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.23","value":"Deut.21.23"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 21:23"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Ps.137.2-4","refs":[{"book":"PSA","endChapter":137,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":137,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Ps.137.2-4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PSA","sourceChapterCoverage":[137],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"111ef632b741d4df39d1488cfc29bcd72dd34e74612f55035222bdb8db1c507c","sourceItemName":"Ps.137.2-4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7383,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"PSA","canonicalOSIS":"Ps","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Ps"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Ps.137.2-4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Ps.137.2-4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Ps.137.5-6","r":"Ps.137.5-6","rawR":"Ps.137.5-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.137.5-6","value":"Ps.137.5-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"137:5-6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The psalmist pledges his loyalty to ",{"c":["Jerusalem"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," the city at the center of God’s reign and the location of fellowship with him."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Ps.137.5-6","refs":[{"book":"PSA","endChapter":137,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":137,"startVerse":5}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Ps.137.5-6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PSA","sourceChapterCoverage":[137],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"01aac4a4e45f4ac7ab979b9bf1191527913e5d0d7f8583f82fb6320b3d7bccc0","sourceItemName":"Ps.137.5-6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7384,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"PSA","canonicalOSIS":"Ps","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Ps"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Ps.137.5-6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Ps.137.5-6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Ps.137.7-9","r":"Ps.137.7-9","rawR":"Ps.137.7-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.137.7-9","value":"Ps.137.7-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"137:7-9"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The poet witnessed the betrayal of God’s people from Judah by ",{"c":["the Edomites"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," so he curses Edom. The long rivalry between Edom and Judah came to a head during Judah’s last days, when the Edomites handed the Judeans over to the Babylonians (",{"href":"?bref=Obad.1.10-11","r":"Obad.1.10-11","rawR":"Obad.1.10-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Obad.1.10-11","value":"Obad.1.10-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Obad 1:10-11"},") and rejoiced in Jerusalem’s fall. • This prayer for vengeance is shocking."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Ps.137.7-9","refs":[{"book":"PSA","endChapter":137,"endVerse":9,"startChapter":137,"startVerse":7}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Ps.137.7-9","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"PSA","sourceChapterCoverage":[137],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"c6cda6b06a093fd3a59ec3873bca7aa04a3f0f3e1de70bbe055eeb9ea869049e","sourceItemName":"Ps.137.7-9","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":7385,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"PSA","canonicalOSIS":"Ps","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Ps"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Ps.137.7-9","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Ps.137.7-9","type":"StudyNote"}],"version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}